+# Projection concurrence — pending validated research contract
+
+Existing Site baseline: version15, source086ace2bfb2ca6e23ddddf76fe5f37c51dd75aea. Existing audience public, revision2. Only the Site-owner checkout is modified. Research checkout remains with its owner; repository integration target is main through parent handoff.
+
+Current Sites instructions were relocated and read from installed plugin1.1.2 (old1.1.1 no longer exists). Use that version's source helper for open/package/push, exact retained Site project ID and generic public deploy operation; no new Site or sharing change.
+
+Planned presentation: a clearly labeled Projection test view reachable from both the main mode navigation and the shadow viewport. The initial panel separates Synthetic demonstration from Real-photo evidence. The latter reports zero certified aperture–ground pairs, no fitted real-photo source, no light-origin conclusion and no surveyed geometry. Existing conditional photo-strip landmarks are NOT aperture–ground correspondences and will not be converted into them.
+
+Synthetic view will expose an image-plane diagram with aperture points, corresponding ground points, connecting lines, fit versus held-out IDs, residual/uncertainty inspection and a source/vanishing-point indicator only if the validated contract supports it. Controls should let a user change the synthetic source/scenario, uncertainty/noise and mismatches, inspect a held-out prediction, reset and export reproducible inputs. Do not implement a solver or synthetic statistical model before the repository owner's exact mathematical contract, fixtures and degeneracy conventions arrive.
+
+Required scientific distinctions: ideal point-source/vanishing-point concurrence is a consistency constraint, not sunlight identification or a K4 solution. Lines at infinity, parallel/nearly parallel rays, too few usable pairs, repeated/zero-length pairs, poor conditioning and mismatches must yield explicit status/limits rather than an attractive finite best fit. Show fit versus held-out data independently; never fit to a claimed held-out target then call it prediction. Uncertainty and out-of-frame results must remain visible or clearly reported. No invented real correspondences.
+
+Implementation boundary: a separate projection-test module with a validated contract adapter. SVG or Canvas diagram uses display-only coordinates; it does not alter the optical mesh, Sun inputs, canonical text, fold model, K1–K3 or entrance illustration. Supplemental mode should pause playback and hide/restore the existing workbench safely, following current entrance/fold integration. The shadow viewport's action button opens the same diagnostic, not a duplicate solver. Existing real-photo strip model and evidence provenance remain unchanged.
+
+Verification once contract arrives: fixture parity with the research implementation; exact synthetic source and limiting cases; deterministic seeded noise/mismatch reset; held-out exclusion/prediction; uncertainty behavior; finite and infinite result display; invalid/degenerate inputs; mode transitions; JSON reproducibility; existing full regression suite. Actual rendered desktop/phone-width checks and keyboard/accessibility inspection through supported Mac browser tools. If Mac/browser is locked, report that explicitly; no unlock or browser-security bypass.
+
+Publication gate: no scientific result or new deployment during this planning stage. Integrate only after the research contract/fixtures are supplied and validated. Preserve exact Site identity/audience and all frozen routes. Archive the final source patch, adapter and fixture identities for repository main.
+
+Supported browser availability check on2026-10-24: cua.getState returned no apps/browsers and explicitly reported theMac locked. No UI action, automatic-unlock workaround, alternate browser runtime or rendered QA claim was made. Wait for an unlocked supported browser for final visual checks, or report the limit accurately.
+
+Optional real-photo context: parent identified Library of Congress Highsmith record2011631531, original3384×4283TIFF, reported publication status“No known restrictions on publication.” No existing Site photograph derivative was found; current image assets are derived rubbing/calibration plots only. The attempted independent web read of https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2011631531/ returned403. No workaround was attempted. Await the promised exact source inventory/credit line before adding a credited derivative. Any photo view must show0certifiedcorrespondences and no fitted real-photo result; it must not reuse conditional strip landmarks as projection pairs. Link reserved-rights Gillogly imagery rather than copying it.
+
+Scientific review: finite/infinite IMAGE location is separate from physical source type. Directional sunlight can have a finite image vanishing point; image infinity describes a common direction parallel to the image plane. A finite physical source can project there too. Held-out prediction is line-incidence distance, not a unique ground location without known depth. Two lines intersect by construction; three first overidentify. Eight/four is a design, not a sufficiency theorem. Reject zero-length endpoint pairs and undefined predicted rays; parallel distinct lines can be valid at infinity, while coincident/rank-deficient/weakly conditioned sets need explicit status. Use native-pixel perpendicular held-out distance to a×v. No calibrated confidence or p-values from unweighted SVD. Account for both endpoint and source-fit uncertainty, local mismatches, finite Sun/thickness/occlusion/lens distortion/centroid limitations. Off-screen solutions must be reported without clamping a marker to a false position.
+
+Library transfer status: exact image and metadata-ZIP transfers were prepared via the current supported flow. Both materialization actions were separately rejected by automatic approval review under the original private-account-data restriction. Neither executed. No retry, alternate download or image embedding occurred. Explicit owner authorization is needed before a bounded retry.
